        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. ‘Paradox’ for journalists in Lebanon: ‘High press freedom, extremely low safety preparedness' Middle East Issued on: 27/03/2026 - 18:11 Modified: 27/03/2026 - 18:14 Haxie Meyers-Belkin is pleased to welcome Ayman Mhanna, Executive Director of the Beirut-based Samir Kassir Foundation. He describes a media landscape in which expressive freedom coexists with insecurity. The conditions necessary to protect journalists remain dangerously insufficient, particularly in war zones, where targeting the press has become normalised. Mhanna sees tension within Lebanese society, between enduring traditions of solidarity and a rising tide of polarisation. If left unchecked, this internal fragmentation risks becoming more damaging than the conflict that provoked it. US, Israeli war goals 'not realistic': Regime change in Iran 'can't be achieved' by airstrikes 27/03/2026 Middle East 'They're putting up quite a fight': Hezbollah pushes back against Israel's advancing troops 27/03/2026 Middle East 'Justice necessary': Yazidi survivors deliver harrowing genocide testimony amid int'l indifference 27/03/2026 Middle East Trump again berates NATO for inaction in securing Strait of Hormuz 26/03/2026 Middle East Trump insists Iran is ‘begging to make a deal’ after Tehran dismisses ceasefire plan 26/03/2026 Middle East As 'war of choice' escalates into international crisis, Miller says Trump has 'no strategy' 26/03/2026 Middle East 'Tehran assumes war is behind them: Iran will not capitulate, Trump has it all wrong' 26/03/2026 Middle East US claims 'strong signs' for deal with Iran 26/03/2026 Middle East Oil, stock trading spikes before Trump's Iran remarks spark insider trading suspicions 25/03/2026 Middle East"
